Title: MIAMI VICE - SALT WATER
Credit: An original story in the season four register
Draft: Storyboard draft

FADE IN:

EXT. MACARTHUR CAUSEWAY - NIGHT - 0214

Heat coming off the asphalt in a rain that hasn't started yet. Palm fronds
shuddering under the streetlights. The causeway empty except for the kind of
traffic that only moves at two in the morning. [[CC: Night traffic; a distant siren; a boat engine idling.]] [[HOLD: 6]]

A furniture truck - HANDS OF THE GULF stencilled in blue on the side - is
stopped at the slow lane with her hazard lights ticking. Nothing wrong with her.
Nothing wrong with anything.

Two cars back, an unmarked Econoline. STAN SWITEK, 40s, heavy, moustache,
sweating through a shirt he put on at six this morning, works a night scope.
LARRY ZITO, 30s, drives with his wrist over the wheel.

ON THE SCOPE - the truck's passenger door. A man gets down with a gym bag.

@SWITEK
That's the third time he's looked at his watch.

@ZITO
He's not looking at his watch. He's looking at the water.

@SWITEK
(in the radio)
Sunrise, this is Sunrise Two. Buy's on the
move. Repeat, buy's on the move.

A TELEPHONE rings in the van - the hard, ugly landline ring. Switek answers,
listens, and slowly lowers the scope.

@SWITEK
(to the radio, flat)
Everybody stand down.

@ZITO
Stand down?

@SWITEK
(to Zito)
It's federal now.

@ZITO
Federal was supposed to tell us at ten.

@SWITEK
Federal tells us when federal feels like it.

Through the rain-streaked glass: the gym bag goes into a cigarette boat idling
in the channel with no running lights. A man in a linen jacket raises a hand
from the cockpit. The boat goes. The truck goes. Both of them calm, both of
them legal, forty thousand dollars of Metro-Dade buy money aboard the second
one. [[CC: A boat engine winding up; traffic resuming.]]

Zito puts the van in gear and does not say anything for a while.

@ZITO
How are we going to write that up?

@SWITEK
Carefully.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - MIAMI BEACH - NIGHT - 0250

Fluorescent light and nobody gone home. A wall of televisions showing nothing.
TRUDY JOPLIN, 20s, at the radio desk with a headset on one ear. GINA
CALABRESE, 20s, pacing with a towel of ice against her elbow.

@GINA
Forty thousand dollars and we get to watch
them drive off.

@TRUDY
We get to watch them drive off so nobody can
say we were the ones who let them.

@GINA
Who's going to say that if nobody knows?

@TRUDY
Everybody. Eventually.

SWITEK and ZITO come in. Nobody asks how it went, which is how they know
everybody already knows.

@SWITEK
Anybody want to tell me what federal's
running at two in the morning that needs
our buy money on it?

@TRUDY
Hands of the Gulf.

@SWITEK
The church people?

@GINA
Nobody's church people, Stan. They're a
charity with sixteen thrift stores and a
warehouse on the river.

@ZITO
(a beat)
That's a lot of thrift stores for a charity
nobody's heard of.

@TRUDY
That's what I said an hour ago.

EXT. DADE COUNTY DOCKS - CONTAINER YARD - NIGHT - 0305

Rain starting properly now. Two hundred blue shipping containers, stacked three
high, stencilled HANDS OF THE GULF - MEDICAL SUPPLIES - DO NOT DELAY. A
watchman's radio playing salsa in an empty office. Gulls asleep on the
light standards.

EDDIE MARIN, 40s, dock foreman, walks the rows with a flashlight and a
clipboard in a plastic sleeve. He is counting. He gets to the end of a row,
looks at the number, and goes back to the start of the same row.

He counts it again. It does not come out the same.

He stops at crate nineteen. He knocks on it with his knuckles. It does not
sound like bandages. It sounds like something packed tight and heavy and
wrapped in plastic.

He knocks again. From inside, faintly - nothing.

He writes something down. The pencil breaks. He looks at the broken point for a
long moment, then puts the pencil in his breast pocket very carefully, like a
man filing evidence. [[CC: A pencil snapping; rain on corrugated steel; a ship's horn.]]

Behind him, a container office door opens. A NIGHT SUPERVISOR, 50s, stands in
the light with a coffee.

@NIGHT SUPERVISOR
Eddie. You're counting again.

@EDDIE
I'm counting.

@NIGHT SUPERVISOR
You counted Tuesday.

@EDDIE
Tuesday came out different.

The night supervisor considers this, and decides it's funny.

@NIGHT SUPERVISOR
Go home, Eddie. Buy your girl something.

@EDDIE
I'll finish the row.

@NIGHT SUPERVISOR
Finish it Monday.

Eddie waits until the office door shuts and the light goes off. Then he goes
back to crate nineteen and writes the number down again.

INT. MARIN HOUSEHOLD - LITTLE HAVANA - NIGHT - 0315

A small house, a tin roof, rain. LUPE MARIN, 12, asleep at the kitchen table
with her homework done and her school bag packed by the door.

On the table beside her: a pay envelope, unopened. A photograph of Eddie with
his arm around her at a quinceañera that hasn't happened yet.

She wakes when a car door slams outside. Nothing. Then a key in the lock.

@LUPE
Papi?

She goes to the door but does not open it. She stands with her hand on the
chain and listens to her father moving around the kitchen: the envelope being
picked up, the fridge, a chair pulled out.

@LUPE
(CONT'D)
Papi, are you counting?

Silence. Then Eddie's voice, through the door, and he is trying to sound like
nothing is wrong, which is how she knows something is.

@EDDIE (O.S.)
Go to bed, mija.

@LUPE
You said if you weren't home -

@EDDIE (O.S.)
I'm home.

A long beat. The envelope goes back down on the table. He sits in the dark
with her homework and does not turn on the light.

@EDDIE (O.S.)
Go to bed, mija. Please.

She goes. He stays at the table with the light off. [[HOLD: 8]]

INT. OCB BULLPEN - CASTILLO'S OFFICE - NIGHT - 0340

LT. MARTIN CASTILLO, 40s, stands at the window with the blinds open on a
parking lot where nothing is happening. He has been standing there a while.

JAMES CROCKETT, 30s, sits in the visitor's chair with five weeks of somebody
else's life on him - sea air, too little sleep, a linen jacket that cost more
than his car.

@CASTILLO
How long have you been Burnett?

@CROCKETT
Six weeks Tuesday.

@CASTILLO
And tonight?

@CROCKETT
Tonight I was on a boat that belonged to a
man who'd have shot me if I'd said no fast
enough. So I said yes. Fast.

@CASTILLO
What did you say yes to?

@CROCKETT
To a number. He wants four hundred thousand
dollars moved by Wednesday. He didn't say
what it was and I didn't ask, because
Burnett doesn't ask, Burnett has a boat in
Fort Lauderdale and a tan and no curiosity
whatsoever.

Castillo turns from the window. Nothing in his face moves.

@CASTILLO
Then we're lucky.

@CROCKETT
We're lucky.

@CASTILLO
We still know which one walked into my
office.

Crockett looks at his hands. They are somebody else's hands today - a heavy
gold watch, a rope bracelet, knuckles he doesn't recognise.

@CROCKETT
Six weeks ago you put me in a jacket.

@CASTILLO
I put a jacket on a detective. The detective
is still in there. He's just harder to see.

A KNOCK. TRUDY at the door with a teletype flimsy.

@TRUDY
Federal's here.

EXT. DOCKS - CONTAINER YARD - NIGHT - 0415

CROCKETT and RICARDO TUBBS, 30s, charcoal suit, collar open, walk the rows with
flashlights. The rain has stopped and left everything dripping. Crate nineteen
has been opened and closed again, badly - the seal is new, the paint around the
hasps is chipped.

@TUBBS
Somebody taught seals how to smile for the
camera.

@CROCKETT
Somebody taught a dock foreman how to count.

@TUBBS
Where's the foreman?

@CROCKETT
Home. Or not.

@TUBBS
You want to stand here all night or you want
to go ask him how he learned?

Tubbs plays his light along the stack. Every crate is the same blue. Nineteen
is the only one glistening differently.

@TUBBS
(CONT'D)
Federal opened it, looked, and closed it
again before we got here. They were in and
out in twenty minutes.

@CROCKETT
That's fast for twenty-two months of work.

@TUBBS
That's fast for anything.

INT. FEDERAL BUILDING - COBURN'S OFFICE - NIGHT - 0445

A government room with a government coffee pot that has been on since Tuesday.
RAY COBURN, 50s, pours. He does not stand up when they come in and he does not
offer them any.

@COBURN
Hands of the Gulf. Relief charity. Sixteen
thrift stores, a warehouse on the river, and
a pipeline to three countries. Every one of
them losing money on paper.

@TUBBS
We've seen the warehouse.

@COBURN
Then you've seen what I let you see.

@CROCKETT
What did you let us see?

@COBURN
Nineteen.

That stops the room. Coburn sips his coffee and lets it.

@COBURN
(CONT'D)
This is a federal case, Detective. Twenty-two
months. Two years of my life in a room with
a television and a manila folder. I don't
need two vice cops stepping on it because
they got up on the wrong side of a beautiful
evening.

@CROCKETT
Twenty-two months and a frightened foreman.

Coburn sets the cup down very carefully.

@COBURN
I'll pretend you didn't say that, because I
have had a longer night than you have.

@TUBBS
Say it anyway.

@COBURN
(to Tubbs)
You're the New York one.

@TUBBS
I'm the one asking.

@COBURN
I have a person inside that organisation. I
have had that person inside for twenty-two
months. Everything I do from here is shaped
around one fact: that person goes home at
the end of this. So. You want to talk about
a foreman who counted crates? Talk. I'll sit
here and drink this coffee and think about
the arithmetic, and the arithmetic is going
to come out the same way it always does.

@CROCKETT
Which is?

@COBURN
You lose the case and I keep the witness. Or
you win the case and I bury the witness. I'd
rather it were neither.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - NIGHT - 0520

The squad at the table with the federal flimsy spread out. Castillo standing.
Trudy typing. Gina at the board, drawing a line from the docks to a bank in
Nassau with a marker that is running out.

@CASTILLO
Mr. Coburn has a witness inside. He wants
them alive more than he wants the case.

@TRUDY
So he's letting it move.

@CASTILLO
He's letting half of it move. That's how you
keep a witness breathing - you give the other
side a reason to think she's useful.

@GINA
She?

Castillo looks at her and does not correct it.

@CASTILLO
That's how I'd do it.

@GINA
And the other half?

@CASTILLO
The other half is ours.

@ZITO
The other half is forty thousand dollars of
ours, Lieutenant.

@CASTILLO
Noted.

@SWITEK
Noted how?

Castillo finally sits down, which is the only answer anybody is getting
tonight.

INT. OCB - HALLWAY - NIGHT - 0530

Crockett at the water fountain with his hands shaking and hating them for it.
Tubbs finds him.

@TUBBS
Burnett drink?

@CROCKETT
Burnett drinks. Crockett's hands do this.

@TUBBS
Since when?

@CROCKETT
Since tonight. Since a man on a boat told me
he liked the way I said yes.

@TUBBS
You can come out.

@CROCKETT
Can I? Six weeks, Rico. I've got a boat
down at the St. Vitus with a name on it
that isn't mine, a telephone number that
rings in an empty apartment, and a woman
in Fort Lauderdale who doesn't exist, and I
talk about her like she's my girlfriend.

@TUBBS
Does it feel like it's working?

@CROCKETT
It feels like it's working.

@TUBBS
Then keep going. I'll be the one who
remembers your name.

EXT. ST. VITUS - MARINA - NIGHT - 0610

Dawn coming up grey over the water. Crockett's boat - the ST. VITUS, a
forty-footer that has seen better owners. He sits on the transom in the linen
jacket. Tubbs comes down the dock with two cups from the all-night place.

@TUBBS
That's Burnett's jacket.

@CROCKETT
Burnett's a guy who owns a boat in Fort
Lauderdale and knows people.

@TUBBS
And you're a guy who owns a boat in Miami
and knows people.

@CROCKETT
Same boat, Rico. Same people.

@TUBBS
No. Same boat. Different people. That's the
whole trick, and you know how it ends.

@CROCKETT
How does it end?

@TUBBS
It ends with the guy in the jacket unable to
remember which name he answers to. I watched
it happen to a man in the Bronx and I am not
watching it again.

They drink. A pelican goes by, low, and does not care about any of it. [[HOLD: 7]]

@CROCKETT
Wednesday he wants four hundred thousand
moved through Nassau.

@TUBBS
Is Nassau a bank?

@CROCKETT
Nassau's a word. He said it like a word.

EXT. LITTLE HAVANA - ALL-NIGHT CAFETERIA - NIGHT - 1940

Chrome and fluorescent light, windows fogged with the cooking. IZZY MORENO,
40s, sits in the window with a cafecito and a cigar he has not lit and will not
light for another six scenes. Crockett and Tubbs sit down opposite him - outside
the glass, on the hood of the car, because that's how this works.

Izzy opens the window two inches.

@IZZY
You're early. Early is suspicious. Late is
Miami. Be late.

@CROCKETT
Blue crates.

@IZZY
Blue crates. Not green, not white. Blue.

@TUBBS
Everybody's got blue crates, Izzy.

@IZZY
Everybody's got blue crates that go out full
and come back fuller? No. That's a charity
with a very generous God.

@CROCKETT
Where do they come back?

@IZZY
A warehouse off the river with a nice sign
and a bad landlord. Sixteen stores feed it.
One truck leaves it at three in the morning
with no lights and comes back at five with
the same weight.

@TUBBS
Same weight.

@IZZY
Same weight, different boxes. That's not
shipping, that's a magic trick.

@CROCKETT
And the bank?

Izzy smiles the smile of a man with something to sell.

@IZZY
A bank that doesn't exist. That's the good
kind, no? You walk in, you say the words, a
man opens a door with no name on it. Ask
them about Salt Water.

@TUBBS
Salt Water.

@IZZY
Salt Water.

@CROCKETT
Whose word?

Izzy shrugs, and for once there is nothing charming in it.

@IZZY
A word doesn't have an owner, so a word
doesn't have a witness. That's why they
picked it.

@CROCKETT
Izzy. Whose word.

@IZZY
A federal word. Now you owe me twice.

He shuts the window and goes back to his cafecito.

INT. SWITEK'S CAR - NIGHT - 2130

Zito drives. Switek counts the money in his wallet twice and puts it back, then
takes it out and counts it again.

@SWITEK
Drop me at Ninth.

@ZITO
Your car's at Ninth.

@SWITEK
I know where my car is, Larry.

Zito drives another block before he says anything.

@ZITO
How much?

@SWITEK
Three.

@ZITO
Three hundred I can get you by Friday.

@SWITEK
Three thousand.

@ZITO
Jesus.

@SWITEK
Don't. Don't do the face. I've seen the face
on my wife, I don't need it from you.

@ZITO
Stan. Three thousand is not a bad week.

@SWITEK
It's not a week. It's a year of Wednesdays.
It's a year of Wednesdays where I told a man
what we were doing before we did it.

Zito takes that in, and keeps driving.

@ZITO
What did he do with it?

@SWITEK
He moved a truck. Twice.

@ZITO
That's what I thought.

@SWITEK
That's what you thought. That's not what I
thought. I thought I was buying time.

EXT. BOOKIE JOINT - BACK ROOM - NIGHT - 2150

A fan turning slowly, a ledger, a rotary telephone, and a man named CUBA, 50s,
who does not need to be cruel and therefore never is.

@CUBA
You're late again, policeman.

@SWITEK
I'm early. You're just open late.

Cuba writes in the ledger and pushes a slip across.

@CUBA
You work Vice. You know who's moving tonight.

@SWITEK
I know who's moving every night.

@CUBA
Then tell me one thing they're not.

Switek looks at the slip for a long time. Somewhere in the front of the house a
radio plays a ball game.

@SWITEK
Warehouse on the river. Nothing before
eleven.

@CUBA
Nothing before eleven.

@SWITEK
Nothing before eleven.

Cuba writes that down too, and Switek watches him write it, and understands
exactly what he has done.

@CUBA
You want a coffee?

@SWITEK
No.

@CUBA
You look like a man who wants a coffee.

@SWITEK
I look like a man who wants to go home.

INT. SWITEK'S APARTMENT - NIGHT - 2240

A rented room with a box fan and a photograph of a girl on the refrigerator.
Switek sits on the bed in his undershirt with the telephone in his lap. He
dials. He hangs up. He dials again.

@SWITEK
(into the telephone)
It's me. No - I know what time it is. I
know what time it is, I just -

He listens. His face does something.

@SWITEK
(CONT'D)
How much is the trip?

He writes on the back of an envelope: 340.

@SWITEK
(CONT'D)
Yeah. Yeah, I can do that. Thursday. I'll
bring it Thursday.

He hangs up and sits with the envelope for a while, then puts it in his wallet
next to nothing at all. [[HOLD: 6]]

INT. OCB - EVIDENCE ROOM - DAY - 0900

Fluorescent. Crate nineteen on a table with the seal cut. Zito in gloves.
Crockett, Tubbs and GINA watching.

Zito lifts the lid. Gauze. Syringes in sealed sleeves. Antibiotics in Spanish.
A carton of children's vitamins.

And underneath, a false bottom, glued and painted to match. And under that -

Nothing.

@ZITO
It was opened. Somebody cleaned it out and
sealed it again and did it better than the
first time.

@GINA
How do you seal a crate better than the
people who sealed it?

@ZITO
You use their machine.

@TUBBS
Federal's machine.

@CROCKETT
Federal's machine, federal's seal, federal's
twenty minutes.

@GINA
So what was in it?

Zito holds up a clear evidence bag with a twist of pale powder out of the
hinge.

@ZITO
Enough to charge somebody. Not enough to
charge anybody we can find.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - DAY - 1000

Castillo assigns it flatly, without ceremony.

@CASTILLO
Gina, Trudy - the thrift chain. Sixteen
stores. Start with the one that pays its
rent in cash. Switek, Zito - the money.
Crockett stays on the water.

@TUBBS
Crockett stays on the water as who?

@CASTILLO
As the man they've already met.

@TUBBS
And me?

@CASTILLO
You stay where he can see you.

@TUBBS
That's not an assignment.

@CASTILLO
It's the only one that matters this week.

EXT. HANDS OF THE GULF THRIFT STORE - CORAL GABLES - DAY - 1100

Coral stucco, a blue awning, a donation bin in the parking lot with a hand-
painted sign: GOD BLESS YOUR DONATION. Heat coming off the asphalt.

TRUDY, in a volunteer's smock, sorts a bin of clothes in the sun. A CLERK, 50s,
watches from the doorway and does not offer to help.

@TRUDY
(to the clerk)
These go to the warehouse too?

@CLERK
Everything goes to the warehouse.

@TRUDY
Even the good stuff?

@CLERK
Especially the good stuff.

@TRUDY
Who sorts it there?

@CLERK
People.

Trudy pulls out a man's jacket with the dry-cleaning tag still on it.

@TRUDY
This is a Brioni.

@CLERK
Somebody died.

@TRUDY
That happens a lot?

The clerk looks at her for the first time.

@CLERK
People die every day, miss. That's why we've
got sixteen stores.

INT. HANDS OF THE GULF - SORTING ROOM - DAY - 1130

A hot room of cardboard and donation smell - wool, mothballs, old perfume. GINA,
in the same smock, lifts boxes onto a belt. She is the only one lifting more
than she has to.

A SUPERVISOR, 30s, checks her clipboard against nothing at all.

@SUPERVISOR
Careful. We inventory on Mondays.

@GINA
What happens on Mondays?

@SUPERVISOR
Mondays we find out what we lost.

@GINA
What do you lose?

@SUPERVISOR
Socks. Mostly socks.

Gina lifts a blue crate down from the stack. It is heavier than bandages have
any right to be. She sets it down, waits two beats, and lifts the corner of the
liner.

Plastic-wrapped brick. She lets the liner fall back.

@GINA
(to the supervisor)
This one's going out?

@SUPERVISOR
Everything goes out.

@GINA
Even the heavy ones?

@SUPERVISOR
Especially the heavy ones.

INT. HANDS OF THE GULF - MARTA'S OFFICE - DAY - 1150

MARTA ESCOBAR, 40s, cream linen, pearls, at a clean desk with three telephones
and a photograph of herself with a bishop. She is rehearsing a donor call into
a dead line, warm and unhurried.

@MARTA
(into the telephone)
Mrs. Abramowitz - yes. Yes, of course. Your
husband's coats went out on Tuesday's truck,
and I want you to know that two hundred
children are warm because of them. That's
not a line, Mrs. Abramowitz. That's a
manifest number. I'll send it to you.

She hangs up, breathes, and picks up the second telephone. The warmth switches
off like a light.

@MARTA
(CONT'D)
(into the telephone)
Tell him if he moves a truck without the
manifest again I will have him arrested
myself.

She hangs up. Then picks up the third telephone and dials one number.

@MARTA
(CONT'D)
(into the telephone)
It's me. Nineteen is off the manifest.
No - I don't care where it is. I care that
it isn't on paper.

She listens. Her face does something small and controlled.

@MARTA
(CONT'D)
Then get it on paper, or get rid of it.

EXT. MIAMI RIVER - CHANNEL - NIGHT - 2100

A cigarette boat with no lights idles under the bridge, engine turning over,
neon sliding on the black water. Burnett steps aboard with a canvas bag. NOEL
PRIETO, 30s, does not shake hands and does not look at the bag.

@PRIETO
You're the one with the bank.

@CROCKETT
I'm the one with the boat.

@PRIETO
Everybody here's got a boat.

@CROCKETT
Everybody here's got a boat they can't take
past Nassau.

Prieto looks at the bag, then at Burnett, then decides something.

@PRIETO
Wednesday. Bring the bank. Leave the badge.

@CROCKETT
What badge?

@PRIETO
The one you don't have.

A long beat on the water. Somewhere upriver a freighter horn.

@CROCKETT
Four hundred thousand.

@PRIETO
Four hundred thousand by Friday, or the bank
goes to somebody with a bigger boat.

@CROCKETT
And what goes in the boat?

Prieto finally smiles.

@PRIETO
Bandages.

INT. THE CARLYLE HOTEL - BAR - NIGHT - 2200

Pastel, a piano, a room full of people who have never been arrested. Burnett
sits at the bar with a drink he is not drinking.

MARTA sits down beside him without asking.

@MARTA
You look like a man at a funeral, Mr.
Burnett.

@CROCKETT
I've been to a lot of those, Mrs. Escobar.

@MARTA
Then you know the trick.

@CROCKETT
What trick?

@MARTA
Nobody asks what the dead man did. They ask
what he left.

She touches his sleeve, lightly, the way a donor touches a cause.

@MARTA
(CONT'D)
We're very grateful for people who give.

@CROCKETT
I haven't given anything yet.

@MARTA
You will. You've got that look. Men with
that look are always generous about
something they can't talk about.

The piano plays something with a lot of chords in it.

@CROCKETT
What do you do with it?

@MARTA
With what?

@CROCKETT
The money people can't talk about.

@MARTA
I put it on a truck.

She sets her glass down, precisely, on a coaster.

@MARTA
(CONT'D)
Wednesday, Mr. Burnett. Bring the bank.

INT. OCB - SURVEILLANCE VAN - NIGHT - SAME - 2200

A van parked across the street from the Carlyle. Trudy and Zito on headsets.
Trudy types what Marta says as she says it.

@TRUDY
She touched him. She never touches anybody.

@ZITO
She touched him because he's money.

@TRUDY
She touched him because she knows.

@ZITO
Knows what?

@TRUDY
I don't know. But she's got a list in her
head of the people she can touch and he just
got on it.

In the back of the van, SWITEK watches a second monitor - the parking lot -
and says nothing at all. He is sweating again.

@ZITO
(CONT'D)
Stan. You want to take the front?

@SWITEK
I'm good back here.

EXT. DOCKS - CONTAINER YARD - NIGHT - 2240

Rain again. A chain-link fence. LUPE climbs it with her school bag and drops
into the yard and goes straight to crate nineteen without hesitating, because
she has been here before with her father.

She knocks three times, the way he did.

Nobody answers. She knocks again, harder.

She puts her ear to the steel. From a container office at the far end, a DOG
barks. She runs.

In the mud by the fence, she drops her school bag. She does not stop for it. [[CC: A dog barking; rain on steel; running footsteps.]]

INT. OCB BULLPEN - DAY - 1000

Trudy at her desk with a stack of printouts and a highlighter. Crockett reads
over her shoulder.

@TRUDY
Seventeen crates came in on the March
shipment and sixteen went out. One was
written off as damaged in transit.

@CROCKETT
Nineteen.

@TRUDY
Nineteen. Again. In June, in August, and
twice in October.

@CROCKETT
Who signs the write-off?

@TRUDY
No name. Just a number.

@CROCKETT
Whose number?

Trudy turns the page around. It's a bank routing number with no bank name
attached.

@TRUDY
That's the thing about a number, Sonny. It
doesn't have to belong to anybody.

INT. MARIN HOUSEHOLD - LITTLE HAVANA - NIGHT - 2330

TUBBS sits at the kitchen table with LUPE. On the table: the pay envelope, the
photograph, and Eddie Marin's wristwatch, which he is not wearing.

@TUBBS
When did he say he'd be home?

@LUPE
He doesn't say. He's home or he isn't.

@TUBBS
Did he say anything about counting?

@LUPE
He said the crates were wrong. He said
nineteen didn't sound right.

@TUBBS
Did he say why?

@LUPE
He said it sounded like somebody was
knocking on the inside.

Tubbs looks at her for a moment.

@TUBBS
Did he say anything else?

She nods. She is not going to cry, and that is somehow worse.

@LUPE
He said if he wasn't home I should give his
watch to a policeman who wasn't scared.

Tubbs looks at the watch for a long moment before he touches it.

@TUBBS
Are you scared?

@LUPE
Yes.

@TUBBS
So am I.

He picks the watch up anyway.

EXT. DOCKS - CONTAINER YARD - NIGHT - 0040

Fire. A container burning from the inside out, the doors bulging, the paint
peeling off in sheets. Fire crews pulling hoses off the tender. Rain and steam
and diesel smoke.

In the glare, the crate number is legible on the side.

Nineteen.

CASTILLO stands in the rain with his hands in his pockets and does not look
away. Crockett and Tubbs stand with him. Nobody says anything for a while. [[CC: Fire; sirens; rain hissing on hot metal; radios.]] [[HOLD: 10]]

A FIRE CAPTAIN comes over with a helmet under his arm.

@FIRE CAPTAIN
Accelerant. Whoever did it wanted what's
inside gone.

@CASTILLO
And the man?

@FIRE CAPTAIN
We'll know in the morning.

@CROCKETT
He was ours.

@CASTILLO
He was a man who counted twice. Get back on
the water so it isn't twice for nothing.

@CROCKETT
Lieutenant -

@CASTILLO
Get back on the water.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - NIGHT - 0130

The team watches the feed of the burned container on the wall televisions.
Trudy types. Gina stands very still with her arms folded. Switek is not here.

@TRUDY
Fire marshal says accelerant. They wanted
the paperwork gone, not the man.

@GINA
They got both.

@TUBBS
No. They got the man. Girl's got the watch.

@GINA
That's not a case, Rico.

@TUBBS
It's a start.

@ZITO
Where's Stan?

Nobody answers that either.

EXT. ST. VITUS - MARINA - NIGHT - 0210

Crockett alone on the boat. Burnett's linen jacket on the rail beside him. He
picks it up, puts it down, picks it up again.

INT. HOSPITAL CHAPEL - NIGHT - 0230

Tubbs sits in a hard chair with LUPE asleep against his shoulder, Eddie's watch
in his pocket. A NURSE passes the door, looks in, and does not come in.

INT. OCB - EVIDENCE CAGE - NIGHT - 0300

Fluorescent light off. A penlight. Switek lets himself in with a key that
still works.

He opens the drawer of buy money. He takes out a stack. He stands there with
his hand on it. [[CC: A drawer sliding; a buzzing fluorescent tube; a radio somewhere in the building.]] [[HOLD: 8]]

He puts half of it back.

He closes the drawer and stands in the dark for a while longer, and then he
leaves, and the door swings shut behind him.

EXT. CAUSEWAY - DAWN - 0540

The sun coming up orange and flat over Biscayne Bay. Crockett drives east into
the light with the windows down and his sleeves rolled. A car telephone rings
beside him. He lets it ring five times.

@CROCKETT
(into the telephone)
Burnett.

INT. HANDS OF THE GULF - MARTA'S OFFICE - DAY - 0900

Cream linen, pearls, the photographs. Burnett sits across the desk with the
canvas bag on his lap.

@MARTA
Mr. Prieto says you have a bank.

@CROCKETT
Mr. Prieto says a lot of things.

@MARTA
He says you can move four hundred thousand
through Nassau without anybody writing it
down. That's not a lot of things. That's one
thing, and it's the only one I need.

She pushes a brochure across the desk. Children on a dock, crates behind them,
a nurse holding up a bandage like a trophy.

@MARTA
(CONT'D)
We ship bandages and antibiotics to three
countries. Last year we shipped eleven tons.
Somebody has been putting things in my
crates, Mr. Burnett.

@CROCKETT
And you'd like to know who.

@MARTA
I'd like to know who, and I'd like it to
stop, and I'd like the two of those to be
the same thing.

@CROCKETT
Why me?

@MARTA
Because you're not from here.

@CROCKETT
Neither are you.

Something flickers behind the pearls and is gone.

@MARTA
No. But I've been here long enough to know
who to ask.

EXT. HANDS OF THE GULF WAREHOUSE - LOADING DOCK - DAY - 0930

Trucks in, trucks out, a chorus of reversing alarms. Burnett walks the dock
with Marta. The blue crates go out on the left and come back on the right, and
come back heavier.

@MARTA
(to a FOREMAN)
Nineteen's off the manifest.

@FOREMAN
Nineteen's gone, ma'am.

@MARTA
Gone where?

@FOREMAN
Gone.

@MARTA
Gone is not a destination, Emilio.

@FOREMAN
It is this morning.

She watches him walk away and then turns to Burnett with the same smile she
uses on donors, except there is nothing behind it.

@MARTA
You see? Sixteen stores, and not one person
in this building can tell me where a crate
went.

@CROCKETT
Maybe they can't.

@MARTA
Everybody can. That's the trouble with
people. They can always tell you. They just
need a reason to.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - DAY - 1100

Trudy spreads sixteen stores' receipts across the table with a highlighter.

@TRUDY
They take in donations all year and report a
loss every year. Not one store turns a
profit and they opened a seventeenth.

@ZITO
Who pays for the seventeenth?

@TRUDY
A trust. Nassau. It's called Salt Water.

@GINA
A trust called Salt Water.

@TRUDY
A trust called Salt Water, and it pays the
lease on a store that sells used toasters
for eleven dollars.

@CROCKETT
Who's the trustee?

@TRUDY
Nobody.

@CROCKETT
Somebody's always the trustee.

@TRUDY
Not on this one. The trustee line is blank.
That's not a mistake. That's architecture.

INT. OCB - INTERROGATION ROOM - DAY - 1300

A DOCK WORKER, 50s, with a union lawyer who has not said a word yet and will
not need to.

@CROCKETT
You signed for crate nineteen twice.

@DOCK WORKER
I signed for everything twice. That's the
job.

@TUBBS
Who told you to sign it the second time?

The dock worker looks at his lawyer, who looks at his nails.

@DOCK WORKER
Nobody tells me anything. I work nights. At
night, nobody tells you anything. That's why
you work nights.

@CROCKETT
Eddie Marin worked nights.

@DOCK WORKER
Eddie counted.

@TUBBS
Counted what?

@DOCK WORKER
Everything. Since March. He'd walk a row
twice and write in a little book, and I told
him: Eddie, don't. And he said: somebody's
got to be able to say what was here.

@CROCKETT
Where's the book?

@DOCK WORKER
Burned, I guess.

@TUBBS
Why do you guess that?

The dock worker finally looks at Tubbs.

@DOCK WORKER
Because a man who counts things in a little
book and then stops counting is a man who
stopped wanting to be able to say.

EXT. OCEAN DRIVE - NIGHT - 2140

Neon on wet street. A black convertible at the kerb with the top down. Crockett
drives. Tubbs reads the dock worker's statement by the dashboard light.

@TUBBS
He's lying about the book.

@CROCKETT
He's lying about the book.

@TUBBS
So Eddie mailed it.

@CROCKETT
Or he put it somewhere a man who counts
things would put it.

@TUBBS
Where's that?

Crockett thinks about it.

@CROCKETT
Somewhere they can't say no to.

EXT. IZZY'S LOT - LITTLE HAVANA - NIGHT - 2130

Izzy under the hood of a Cadillac with a rag and an opinion. Crockett leans on
the fender.

@IZZY
Salt Water is not a bank. Salt Water is a
word. You go to a bank and you say Salt
Water and a man opens a door with no name on
it.

@CROCKETT
Whose word?

@IZZY
Whoever's. That's the beauty. A word doesn't
have an owner, so a word doesn't have a
witness.

@CROCKETT
Izzy. Whose word.

Izzy shrugs, and for once there is nothing charming in it.

@IZZY
A federal word.

@CROCKETT
Federal.

@IZZY
Federal. I know a man who drove a truck for
a federal word for two years, and then one
day the word stopped answering the telephone
and the man was just a man with a truck.

@CROCKETT
Where's the man now?

@IZZY
Driving a truck. What else?

EXT. IZZY'S LOT - NIGHT - LATER - 2210

The lot is dark now. Izzy has gone inside. Crockett's car pulls out.

And across the street, a parked sedan that has been there since before Crockett
arrived finally starts. In the passenger seat, RAY COBURN lowers a pair of
binoculars and writes in a notebook.

@COBURN
(to the driver)
Run the plate.

@DRIVER
It's a vice plate, Ray.

@COBURN
I know it's a vice plate. Run it anyway.
Then find out who the boat belongs to.

The driver writes. Coburn looks at the empty lot for a long time.

@COBURN
(CONT'D)
Burnett.

@DRIVER
Sir?

@COBURN
Nothing.

He closes the notebook.

@COBURN
(CONT'D)
Twenty-two months.

@DRIVER
Sir?

@COBURN
Drive.

INT. OCB - CASTILLO'S OFFICE - DAY - 0900

Tubbs standing. Crockett sitting. Castillo doing neither - he is at the window
again, and everybody in the room has noticed the pattern by now.

@TUBBS
You're sending him in with four hundred
thousand dollars of money we can't account
for, to buy drugs that don't exist, from a
woman whose crates are already on fire.

@CASTILLO
The money we can account for.

@TUBBS
Not my objection.

@CASTILLO
It's a good one. Write it down and put it in
the file so when this goes bad, somebody
knows we knew.

@TUBBS
I'd rather stop it going bad.

@CASTILLO
So would I.

@CROCKETT
It won't go bad. I'm very charming.

@TUBBS
Burnett's charming. That's the problem.
Charm's the thing that works on a woman and
then works on you.

@CASTILLO
(to Crockett)
What did she say when you left?

@CROCKETT
Who?

@CASTILLO
Mrs. Escobar.

A beat. Crockett was not expecting to be asked that, and the room can tell.

@CROCKETT
She asked me what I do with money people
can't talk about.

@CASTILLO
And?

@CROCKETT
I told her I put it on a truck.

@CASTILLO
Did you?

@CROCKETT
Yes.

@CASTILLO
Good. That's the true answer and the useful
one. Keep giving her that one.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - DAY - 0930

Four hundred thousand dollars in a duffel bag on Trudy's desk, in bands. Zito
whistles. Gina does not find it funny.

@TRUDY
It's from three different seizures and a
forfeiture that closes in April. If we lose
it, that's not a budget problem, that's a
grand jury.

@ZITO
If we lose it I'll get a job at the track.

@GINA
You already have a job at the track.

@ZITO
Then I'll go full time.

@CASTILLO
(to Trudy)
Count it twice.

@TRUDY
Yes, Lieutenant.

@CASTILLO
And log every band number, because if
Coburn's plane is on the ground with our
money on it, I want to be able to prove it
was ours all the way to a committee room.

@GINA
And if it's on the ground and he says it was
his?

Castillo picks the bag up and hands it to Crockett.

@CASTILLO
Then we're two policemen with a bag and a
story. Don't let that happen.

EXT. MARINA - NIGHT - 2030

Zito and Trudy hand off the canvas bag in the dark behind the bait shop.
Across the channel, a parked sedan with two men in it who are not pretending to
read anything.

@ZITO
That's the second time tonight.

@TRUDY
That's not us being followed. That's
somebody else being followed, and they
brought a friend to watch us watch.

@ZITO
Coburn's.

@TRUDY
Coburn's, or the other half's. Which is the
trouble with letting half of something
through. Sooner or later you can't tell
which half you are.

Zito looks at the sedan until the sedan looks back.

@ZITO
You ever think about what happens to the
other half?

@TRUDY
Every night.

@ZITO
Me too.

INT. MIAMI BANK - PRIVATE ROOM - DAY - 1000

Burnett with a TRUST OFFICER, 50s, and a form. No nameplate on the desk. A
window with the blinds shut on a beautiful day.

@TRUST OFFICER
And the source of funds?

@CROCKETT
Marine salvage.

@TRUST OFFICER
(charming)
Everybody here's in marine salvage.

He stamps the form, then pauses with the stamp in the air.

@TRUST OFFICER
(CONT'D)
Is there anything else?

@CROCKETT
Salt Water.

The trust officer sets the stamp down as if it had bitten him. He opens a
different drawer. He takes out a plain white card with a number on it and
slides it across without touching it twice.

@TRUST OFFICER
That's the routing. There's no name on it
because there's no name to put.

@CROCKETT
Whose bank is this?

@TRUST OFFICER
It's a bank.

@CROCKETT
And if I want to see the account?

@TRUST OFFICER
Then you'll need the word, and you've said
the word, so you don't need me any more.

INT. BANK - VAULT CORRIDOR - DAY - 1015

Burnett is walked down a corridor by a man in a grey suit who says nothing at
all. At the end, a door. No name on it. The man opens it, stands aside, and
does not follow him in.

Inside: a room with a table, a scale, and a woman in her fifties with reading
glasses and a ledger. She does not look up.

@WOMAN
Amount.

@CROCKETT
Four hundred thousand.

She writes. Still not looking up.

@WOMAN
Routing.

He slides the card across. She reads the number, and for the first time she
looks at him, and it is the least friendly thing anybody has done to him all
week.

@WOMAN
Who gave you this?

@CROCKETT
A man at a desk.

@WOMAN
There's no man at a desk.

@CROCKETT
There was this morning.

She considers him for a long moment, then closes the ledger.

@WOMAN
Wednesday. Bring it in bands, not in a bag.
And don't come back here with a policeman.

@CROCKETT
I'm not a policeman.

@WOMAN
Everybody's a policeman.

EXT. FLAGLER DOG TRACK - NIGHT - 2130

Cigarette smoke, losing tickets underfoot, a race nobody is watching. Switek
stands at the rail with a program he has already torn in half and a bet he has
already lost.

The dogs go past. He does not look up.

INT. DOG TRACK - CLUBHOUSE BAR - NIGHT - LATER

Zito finds him. Switek is up two hundred. Then he isn't. Then it's worse than
that.

@ZITO
How much of it's ours?

@SWITEK
None of it's ours. That's why I borrowed it.

@ZITO
You took buy money.

@SWITEK
I took a stack out of a drawer I already
took a stack out of, and I put half of it
back, and now it's a hole with my name on it
either way, so I'm trying to fill it before
anybody measures it.

Zito sits down carefully, like the chair might be evidence.

@ZITO
Stan. You tell Castillo tonight or I do.

@SWITEK
You won't.

@ZITO
No. I won't.

@SWITEK
Then what are we doing here?

@ZITO
I'm sitting with you. That's what I'm doing.
That's all I've got left, Stan.

Switek signals the barman for two more, and pays for them with a twenty that
has a band number on it if you know to look.

EXT. DOG TRACK - PARKING LOT - NIGHT - 2310

Switek walking to his car across a lot of dead light standards. A man - MORALES,
30s, expensive shoes, a face built for weddings - falls in beside him.

@MORALES
Detective Switek.

@SWITEK
I'm off the clock.

@MORALES
Everybody's always on somebody's clock.

@SWITEK
What do you want?

@MORALES
My employer has an interest in a warehouse
on the river. My employer would like to know
when that warehouse is quiet.

@SWITEK
Tell your employer to call the warehouse.

@MORALES
My employer has. The warehouse says it's
quiet all the time. My employer would prefer
a second opinion.

Morales puts an envelope on the hood of Switek's car and taps it once.

@MORALES
Two thousand. For one telephone call.

@SWITEK
Three thousand's my problem and two's your
solution. You didn't do the arithmetic on
that, did you?

@MORALES
I did. That's why it's two.

Switek looks at the envelope. He looks at it for a very long time. Then he
picks it up and puts it in his pocket.

@SWITEK
Tell your employer the warehouse is quiet
until Friday.

@MORALES
And after Friday?

@SWITEK
After Friday I don't know what anybody's
doing.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - NIGHT - 2330

Gina, still in the volunteer's smock, plays back a camcorder tape on a
television with the sound off. Blue crates. A truck leaving at 0300 with no
lights. A man in the yard who is not a dock worker.

@GINA
They don't move at night. They move at three
in the morning, which is a different thing
entirely, because at three in the morning
nobody's on the clock but the people who
want to be.

@TRUDY
Seventeen stores. One warehouse. Six trucks.

@GINA
One truck. I've watched it six times. Same
truck, six nights, and it never goes to the
warehouse on the sign.

@TRUDY
Where does it go?

@GINA
North. There's a strip up past the water
management district where the road ends, and
I'd like to know what ends with it.

@TRUDY
You want to tell Castillo?

@GINA
I want to tell Castillo when I can tell him
what's at the end of the road. Otherwise
he'll ask me how I know and I'll say six
nights, and he'll say that's not enough, and
he'll be right.

EXT. THRIFT STORE - CORAL GABLES - NIGHT - 0300

The strip mall dead. One security light. Gina and Trudy in a car across the
street with a thermos and a camcorder on the dash.

@TRUDY
That's the truck.

@GINA
That's the truck.

The box truck rolls in with its lights off, backs to the donation bay, and two
men load four blue crates in ninety seconds without speaking.

Then the driver does something odd: he checks the street, and he checks it
slowly, the way a man checks a street when he has been told to.

@GINA
He's been told somebody's watching.

@TRUDY
Or he's careful.

@GINA
Careful's fine. Told is bad.

Trudy gets the plate on the camcorder. Gina watches the driver watch the
street.

@TRUDY
(to herself)
Got you.

@GINA
Not yet.

EXT. WAREHOUSE - ROOFTOP ACROSS THE CANAL - NIGHT - 0310

Crockett and Tubbs on a gravel roof with a night scope and a thermos. The truck
backs to a door that opens from the inside. Two crates come off.

And one man - a mule, young, in a windbreaker - is walked behind the truck by
two others and does not come back around.

@TUBBS
Say it.

@CROCKETT
I saw it.

@TUBBS
Say the other part.

@CROCKETT
The part where I don't do anything because
if I do, a dead foreman was for nothing.

@TUBBS
That's the part.

@CROCKETT
Twenty-two months, Rico. She's been in there
twenty-two months.

@TUBBS
I know.

@CROCKETT
And if I go down there now, I get one man
and she gets a grave.

They watch until the truck is gone and the yard is still again. [[CC: A truck engine fading; a dog in the distance; water slapping a seawall.]] [[HOLD: 8]]

@TUBBS
Somebody has to be able to say what was
here.

@CROCKETT
Eddie said that.

@TUBBS
Eddie had it right.

EXT. WAREHOUSE - CANAL - NIGHT - 0340

Crockett and Tubbs along the seawall with flashlights on the mud. Tubbs finds a
pay stub in a plastic sleeve, face down, trodden in.

@TUBBS
Alberto Ruiz. Twenty-four. Eighty dollars
for a night's work, and the stub says
HANDS OF THE GULF.

@CROCKETT
He signed for it.

@TUBBS
He signed for eighty dollars.

Crockett finds a spent casing in the gravel, picks it up with a handkerchief,
and looks at it in the beam.

@CROCKETT
Nine.

@TUBBS
Everybody's got a nine.

@CROCKETT
Everybody's got a nine they don't fire in a
city, Rico. You fire it where nobody hears
it. That's why they walk them behind the
truck here.

INT. MEDICAL EXAMINER'S OFFICE - DAY - 0900

Cold light, steel. The M.E., 60s, pulls a drawer. Tubbs and Crockett stand
with their hands in their pockets.

@M.E.
Marin, Eduardo. Forty-two. Died of smoke
inhalation before the fire reached him.

@TUBBS
Before.

@M.E.
Before. He was alive when it started.

@CROCKETT
And the arm?

@M.E.
Broken three days ante-mortem. Spiral
fracture of the humerus. Somebody held him
and turned him.

@TUBBS
Held him.

@M.E.
Somebody with two hands and no hurry.

@CROCKETT
Anything on the hands?

@M.E.
Graphite on the right palm, and a splinter
under the nail of the index finger. From a
pencil, I'd say.

@TUBBS
He was still counting.

@M.E.
He was still counting.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - DAY - 0930

Castillo and Coburn at the board. Coburn has not slept in two days and has
stopped pretending otherwise.

@COBURN
I have a woman inside that warehouse who
names names, and every crate you stop is a
crate she has to explain. You want the
warehouse? Fine. Take it. Take it after
she's out.

@CASTILLO
She's been inside twenty-two months.

@COBURN
She's been inside twenty-two months because
dead informants don't testify and I am not
going to explain another one to a committee.

@CASTILLO
Tell me her name.

@COBURN
No.

@CASTILLO
Then tell me how she gets out.

@COBURN
Wednesday night. There's a flight. When it's
wheels-up she's a witness and you can have
the whole building.

@CASTILLO
And if there's no flight?

@COBURN
Then there's no witness and you'd better
have a very good reason for whatever you did
next.

Coburn picks up his coffee and finds it cold, and drinks it anyway.

EXT. ST. VITUS - MARINA - DAY - 1100

Tubbs finds Crockett scrubbing the deck of a boat he does not own.

@TUBBS
You haven't slept.

@CROCKETT
Burnett doesn't sleep. Burnett's got a place
in Fort Lauderdale with a bar in it and a
woman who doesn't ask what he does.

@TUBBS
Does she?

@CROCKETT
She doesn't exist, Rico. That's the part
you're not getting. There's no apartment.
There's a telephone number and a jacket and
six weeks of me talking in a voice that
isn't mine.

He drops the brush in the bucket.

@CROCKETT
(CONT'D)
Somebody's got to be him or nobody is.

@TUBBS
Somebody's got to be you, Sonny. That's the
job I've got.

INT. ST. VITUS - CABIN - DAY - 1115

Below decks: a bunk, a hotplate, a telephone that is not connected to anything.
A tape recorder on the table. Crockett sits down, presses record, and does not
know what to say for a while.

@CROCKETT
(into the recorder)
Tuesday. It's Tuesday. My name is James
Crockett. I'm a detective with Metro-Dade
Vice, Organized Crime Bureau. My badge
number is -

He stops. He says the rest of it anyway.

@CROCKETT
(CONT'D)
My badge number is 4-1-1-9. I have a boat.
The boat is evidence. There's a woman in
Fort Lauderdale named -

He stops again.

@CROCKETT
(CONT'D)
There isn't a woman in Fort Lauderdale. I
made her up on a Tuesday in a bar because a
man asked me what I did for a living and the
truth wasn't going to work on him.

He listens to the tape hiss.

@CROCKETT
(CONT'D)
If you're listening to this and you don't
know which one you are, call Tubbs. He
knows.

INT. CARLYLE HOTEL - SUITE - NIGHT - 2100

Marta pours coffee. Outside, the piano plays the same song it played last week.
Burnett puts the briefcase on the table and does not open it.

@MARTA
Four hundred thousand.

@CROCKETT
Four hundred thousand. Nassau, then the
island, then back through the warehouse
you've never seen.

@MARTA
I've seen it. I sign the lease.

@CROCKETT
Then you know what's in nineteen.

Marta sets the pot down and looks at him with the interest of a woman deciding
whether a man is useful or merely charming.

@MARTA
I know what's not in nineteen. That's the
only inventory that ever mattered.

@CROCKETT
And the man who counted it?

@MARTA
People who count things are always the first
thing a charity loses.

@CROCKETT
That's a cold thing to say about a dead man.

@MARTA
It isn't cold. It's accounting. Sixteen
stores and eleven tons of bandages, Mr.
Burnett. Do you know what a single
investigation costs a charity? Not in money.
In children who don't get a thing that was
already on a boat.

@CROCKETT
So you don't want to know who's doing it.

@MARTA
I want to know who's doing it. I want to
know on a Wednesday, after the money moves,
with a lawyer on a retainer and a statement
already typed.

She touches the briefcase.

@MARTA
(CONT'D)
That's the difference between you and me.
You think the truth is a thing you find. I
know it's a thing you schedule.

EXT. CARLYLE HOTEL - STREET - NIGHT - 2140

Crockett gets into the car where Tubbs and Trudy are waiting with the headsets.
He does not start the engine.

@TRUDY
You defended her.

@CROCKETT
I did what?

@TRUDY
When she said the dead man was accounting.
You let it go. Burnett wouldn't let that go.
Burnett would have said something ugly.

@TUBBS
She's right.

@CROCKETT
She said sixteen stores and eleven tons.

@TUBBS
And you believed it.

@CROCKETT
I believed she believed it.

@TUBBS
That's worse.

@CROCKETT
Yeah. I know.

He starts the engine.

@CROCKETT
(CONT'D)
(to Trudy)
Did you get the part about the flight?

@TRUDY
Wednesday. She said Wednesday twice.

@CROCKETT
Coburn said Wednesday.

@TUBBS
Everybody's saying Wednesday.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - NIGHT - 2330

Trudy, three screens deep into the charity's donation database, stops typing.

@TRUDY
(to Zito)
Eddie Marin wasn't a foreman. He was a
donor.

@ZITO
Dock workers don't donate.

@TRUDY
He donated eleven dollars a month for nine
years. And last month he donated a page.

She turns the screen around. A scanned donation envelope. Taped into the corner:
a photocopy of a handwritten column of numbers.

@TRUDY
(CONT'D)
Nineteen. Thirty-one. Forty. And a bank
routing number that isn't a bank.

@ZITO
He mailed it to them.

@TRUDY
He mailed it to the one place that couldn't
throw it away.

@ZITO
Nine years, eleven dollars a month.

@TRUDY
Nine years, eleven dollars a month, so that
when he put the page in the envelope, the
envelope got opened.

EXT. LITTLE HAVANA - BODY SHOP - NIGHT - 0040

Izzy's cousin, TOÑO, 20s, slides under a refrigerated truck with a magnetic box
and a rag. Crockett watches the street.

@IZZY
You know what he charges? Nothing. He owes
me. Everybody owes me. This is why I am a
very wealthy man with no money.

@CROCKETT
If that box is found -

@IZZY
It won't be found. It'll fall off in traffic
like everything else in this city.

@CROCKETT
If it is found, he's dead.

Izzy looks at his cousin under the truck, and the joke goes out of him.

@IZZY
Then you'd better be right about Wednesday.

INT. OCB - EVIDENCE ROOM - DAY - 0900

Zito opens the truck's maintenance logbook, which came in with the tow. He
reads, then reads again, then looks at the locker row.

He opens SWITEK'S locker. Inside: a folded racing form and three bet slips
with the same bookie's stamp.

He closes it and stands there with his hand on the door. [[CC: A metal locker door; a radio somewhere in the building; a dog barking outside.]]

INT. OCB - LOCKER ROOM - DAY - 0910

Switek shaving with a travel razor and a paper towel. Zito comes in, closes the
door, and puts the bet slips on the bench between them.

@SWITEK
You went in my locker.

@ZITO
I went in your locker.

@SWITEK
You're my partner.

@ZITO
That's why it was my locker too.

Switek picks up the slips. He looks at them like they belong to somebody else
and he's been asked to identify the body.

@SWITEK
Three hundred a week. Eight months.

@ZITO
Say the rest.

@SWITEK
Last night a man in a parking lot gave me
two thousand dollars to tell him when a
warehouse is quiet.

Zito goes completely still.

@ZITO
Did you?

@SWITEK
I told him it's quiet until Friday.

@ZITO
You told him.

@SWITEK
I told him it's quiet until Friday, which is
a lie, and I took two thousand dollars for a
lie, and I don't know whose lie it was.

@ZITO
Whose man was he?

@SWITEK
He said employer. He had shoes that cost
more than my car.

Zito sits down on the bench.

@ZITO
Stan. This ends today.

@SWITEK
It ends Wednesday. Let me get to Wednesday.

@ZITO
Why Wednesday?

@SWITEK
Because my daughter's school trip is
Thursday and I have already got the money
for it, and I would like to have given it to
her before I lose the job that pays for the
rest of her life.

EXT. EVERGLADES - TWO-LANE - NIGHT - 2330

A refrigerated truck heading west into nothing. Burnett drives. Prieto rides
with a pistol on the seat between them, on top of a folded map, where both of
them can see it.

@PRIETO
You want to know why it's on the seat?

@CROCKETT
I assumed you forgot your holster.

@PRIETO
It's on the seat because a man with a gun in
his pocket has decided something. A man with
a gun on the seat hasn't.

@CROCKETT
That's very philosophical for a Wednesday.

@PRIETO
It's not Wednesday yet.

They drive. Cicadas. The truck's cooling unit cycling on and off behind them.

@PRIETO
(CONT'D)
Tell me about the woman.

@CROCKETT
What woman?

@PRIETO
Escobar.

@CROCKETT
She signs the lease.

@PRIETO
She signs the lease and she doesn't know.

@CROCKETT
Doesn't know what?

Prieto picks up the pistol, checks the magazine, puts it back on the seat.

@PRIETO
Everybody knows. That's the thing about a
charity, my friend. Everybody knows and
everybody's grateful.

@CROCKETT
Are you grateful?

@PRIETO
I'm not in it for the bandages.

EXT. EVERGLADES - AIRSTRIP - NIGHT - 0110

A DC-3 on a grass strip with its engines turning, strobe lighting the sawgrass.
Crates going aboard on a conveyor. The refrigerated truck backed to the door.

And at the treeline, two men with a shovel and a shape wrapped in tarpaulin.

@PRIETO
Don't look at that.

@CROCKETT
I'm not looking at that.

@PRIETO
Good. Nobody looks at that. That's how you
get to keep looking at everything else.

Crockett does not look. He reads the tail number off the fuselage in the strobe
light instead, and says it to himself twice, the way Eddie Marin would have. [[CC: Aircraft engines; insects; a shovel in dirt; a generator putt-putting.]]

EXT. EVERGLADES - AIRSTRIP - NIGHT - 0150

The truck bumps back toward the highway. Burnett at a payphone outside a closed
gas station, calling a number he has called six times.

@CROCKETT
(into the phone)
It's me. I've got a tail number. N -
November, four, two, seven, Mexico. Say it
back.

He listens.

@CROCKETT
(CONT'D)
Say it back, Rico.

@TUBBS (V.O.)
(filtered)
N427 Mexico. You sound like somebody else.

@CROCKETT
I know.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - DAWN - 0530

Trudy at the teletype, reading aloud as it comes off the machine. The squad
standing around her in the dark.

@TRUDY
Registered to a holding company in Nassau
that was dissolved in 1981. And the
operating certificate was issued by -

She stops. Nobody speaks. Castillo takes the paper and turns it around himself.

@CASTILLO
Issued by us.

@GINA
By us?

@CASTILLO
By the United States government.

@ZITO
So we're not chasing a charity.

@TUBBS
We're chasing a case.

@CASTILLO
We're chasing a case that has been running
itself for two years.

INT. FEDERAL BUILDING - COBURN'S OFFICE - DAY - 1000

Coburn pours two cups and does not offer one. Crockett stands. Tubbs sits.
Castillo stands by the door with his hands behind him and says nothing at all.

@COBURN
The plane is mine. The strip is mine. Half
the crates are mine.

@TUBBS
And the other half?

@COBURN
Theirs. Because if all of it stops, she's
dead, and if half of it stops, she's a
witness with a story nobody can disprove.

@CROCKETT
Eddie Marin counted your crates and burned
for it.

@COBURN
Eddie Marin counted my crates, yes. And I
told him to stop. I told him twice.

@CROCKETT
When?

@COBURN
The second time was four days before the
fire.

The room goes very quiet. Coburn finally sits down, and he looks older doing it.

@COBURN
(CONT'D)
You want to arrest me? Arrest me. Take me in
front of a judge who'll ask what I was
protecting, and I'll say a name, and she
dies on a Tuesday. Then you've got me.

@TUBBS
What's the arithmetic on the other half?

@COBURN
The other half is eleven tons of somebody
else's bandages and about nine hundred
kilos, and I have let every one of those
kilos through for twenty-two months so that
on Wednesday a woman can walk into a
courtroom and say the word Salt Water out
loud.

@CROCKETT
And Alberto Ruiz?

@COBURN
Who?

@CROCKETT
Twenty-four years old. Eighty dollars a
night. Walked behind a truck and didn't come
back around.

Coburn writes the name down on a pad, and that may be the worst thing he does
all day.

@COBURN
I didn't know that one.

INT. OCB - CASTILLO'S OFFICE - DAY - 1100

@CASTILLO
We take the warehouse. We take it with his
plane on the ground and his witness out.

@TUBBS
That's not in our jurisdiction.

@CASTILLO
That's not in anybody's jurisdiction. That's
why we're the ones who'll do it.

@CROCKETT
And Coburn?

Castillo looks at him for a long moment.

@CASTILLO
Coburn is a policeman.

@TUBBS
So was the man who held Eddie Marin's arm.

@CASTILLO
Yes.

EXT. FEDERAL BUILDING - GARAGE - DAY - 1200

Castillo, alone, watches from the ramp as COBURN comes down to a row of lockers
in the empty garage, puts a manila envelope in Number 12, and closes it.

Coburn stands there for a moment with his hand on the door. Then he looks
straight up at the ramp.

Castillo does not move. Coburn does not either.

After a while, Coburn leaves. Castillo walks down to Locker 12, puts his hand
on the door, and does not open it.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - DAY - 1230

Castillo at the board. The squad around the table.

@CASTILLO
Wednesday. Prieto moves the money at
eleven. The plane is wheels-up at one.
Coburn's witness comes out before that, and
I don't know how, and I'm not asking.
Warehouse, eleven o'clock.

@TRUDY
And if the witness doesn't come out?

@CASTILLO
Then we do it anyway.

@GINA
And Coburn?

@CASTILLO
Coburn is a policeman. Somebody hold that
thought for me until Wednesday.

EXT. ELEMENTARY SCHOOL - MIAMI - DAY - 1430

Lupe stands at a chain-link fence with her school bag. Across the street, in a
car, Prieto sits with the window down. She goes completely still.

@TUBBS
Is that him?

@LUPE
That's the man who gave my father money.

@TUBBS
Did he hurt you?

She doesn't answer. She does not have to.

@LUPE
He gave my father a bag. My father gave it
back. My father said he didn't want to carry
it, and the man laughed and said carrying
was the easy part.

@TUBBS
What's the hard part?

@LUPE
Counting.

INT. COUNTY - FAMILY SERVICES - DAY - 1600

A small office with a box of tissues and a poster about reporting abuse. Tubbs
with LUPE and a SOCIAL WORKER, 30s, who is doing her best.

@SOCIAL WORKER
Mr. Tubbs, I have to ask. Is there family?

@TUBBS
There's an aunt in Hialeah.

@SOCIAL WORKER
And?

@TUBBS
And the aunt's husband is a man who used to
hit her, and the girl says she'd rather be
anywhere.

@SOCIAL WORKER
That's not how this works.

@TUBBS
That's how this works with a witness.

@SOCIAL WORKER
She's twelve years old, Detective. She's not
a witness. She's a child whose father was
burned in a box.

Lupe, very quiet:

@LUPE
My father counted things.

@SOCIAL WORKER
I'm sorry?

@LUPE
He counted things so somebody could say what
was there. Somebody has to be able to say
what was there.

The social worker looks at Tubbs. Tubbs looks at his hands.

@TUBBS
(to the social worker)
Then let her say it.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - DAY - 1700

Zito closes the door. Switek knows why before he says anything.

@SWITEK
Cuba.

@ZITO
Cuba's book. And Cuba's book settles through
an account that pays the warehouse's
insurance, Stan. You've been handing a
charity our movements for eight months.

@SWITEK
I've been handing a bookie three hundred
dollars a week to keep my ex-wife from
finding out I'm a man who bets on dogs.

Zito waits.

@SWITEK
(CONT'D)
Eight months.

@ZITO
Say it to Castillo.

@SWITEK
Wednesday.

@ZITO
Wednesday's gone, Stan. There's a dead man
and a burned crate and a girl in a county
office who says her father counted things.
Wednesday's gone.

Switek sits down.

@SWITEK
Then tell him. You tell him. I'll sit here
and I'll write it all down and sign it. But
you tell him, because I can't watch his face
do that.

INT. GINA'S APARTMENT - NIGHT - 1900

Small, neat, a fan in the window. Gina on the bed with her sling off. Trudy at
the table, laying out a dress on the bedspread.

@TRUDY
Borrowed. From my cousin. She's bigger than
you, so pin the shoulder.

@GINA
I can't wear somebody's cousin's dress into
a hotel full of men who know what their own
crates weigh.

@TRUDY
You can and you will.

@GINA
Trudy.

@TRUDY
What.

@GINA
If I go in there and something happens, I
want you to write down that I said the
truck left at three, because if I don't come
back out, that's the only thing that makes
it mean anything.

@TRUDY
Come back out.

@GINA
Write it down.

Trudy writes it down on the back of an envelope, and then she sits on the bed
next to her and doesn't say anything for a while.

EXT. BOOKIE JOINT - BACK ROOM - NIGHT - 2015

Switek wired, sweating, trying to be a man having a normal conversation with a
man who has stopped believing him.

@SWITEK
They're moving tonight. Warehouse. Eleven.

@CUBA
Eleven you told me on Tuesday. Eleven you
told me in March.

@SWITEK
Eleven's tonight.

@CUBA
You know, Stan. I have liked you for a long
time. You bet like a man who needs to lose,
and I have never once had to send anybody to
your house, and I appreciate that. A bookie
appreciates a customer who pays.

@SWITEK
Cuba -

Cuba opens a drawer that does not contain money.

@CUBA
I think you're wearing a wire, and I think
that's braver than I expected, and I think
somebody's going to come in that door now.

EXT. ALLEY BEHIND THE BOOKIE JOINT - NIGHT - 2022

Zito comes through the door with his gun out and gets Switek into the car. A
shot takes Zito high in the shoulder and puts him against the fender.

@SWITEK
Larry.

@ZITO
Drive, Stan.

@SWITEK
You're hit.

@ZITO
Stan. Drive.

Switek drives. Zito sits in the passenger seat with his hand over it, and
swears exactly once, quietly, like a man in a library.

@ZITO
(CONT'D)
Call it in. Call it in and say my name.

@SWITEK
They'll know it was you.

@ZITO
Good. Say my name.

EXT. HOTEL MIRAMAR - POOLSIDE - NIGHT - 2200

Pink neon on water, a party Burnett paid for, a band playing a cover of
something. GINA, in the borrowed dress, works the far side of the pool with a
glass and a smile that does not reach her eyes.

PRIETO watches her the way a man watches a door he has already checked.

Burnett watches Prieto watch her, and cannot move without saying who he is.

In the van across the street: TRUDY on the headset, ZITO bandaged and sitting
upright, CASTILLO standing behind both of them.

@TRUDY
(into the radio)
He's on her. He's been on her since she came
in.

@CASTILLO
(into the radio)
What's she doing?

@TRUDY
Nothing. That's the problem. She's doing
nothing and he's still on her.

@ZITO
He's not on her because of what she's doing.
He's on her because of what she's wearing.
Nobody at this party wears a borrowed dress.

INT. HOTEL MIRAMAR - SERVICE CORRIDOR - NIGHT - 2245

Concrete, a linen cart, a green exit sign. Gina goes through a door to the
loading bay and two men take her by the arms, and she does not get a sound out
before a hand goes over her mouth.

One of them says something into a radio, and walks her toward a van with the
side door already open.

EXT. HOTEL MIRAMAR - LOADING BAY - NIGHT - 2247

A van with the side door open. From the pool deck thirty feet away, the band
starts something fast and the party laughs.

Crockett comes around the corner with a bottle in a bag, stops, and has
approximately two seconds to decide who he is. [[CC: A party on the other side of a wall; a van door sliding; a radio squelching.]] [[HOLD: 8]]

He puts the bottle down. He goes in.

What follows is fast, ugly and not clever. He puts the first man into the van
door. The second one hits him twice in the ribs and he goes down on the asphalt
and gets up with a length of pipe off the dock.

It ends with the second man on the ground and not getting up quickly.

Gina is on her knees being sick into the gutter.

@CROCKETT
(into his radio)
Burnett's burned. Repeat, Burnett's burned.
Officer down, send it all.

He sits down on the kerb beside her and puts his head back against the wall.

@GINA
You stupid son of a bitch.

@CROCKETT
I know.

@GINA
Six weeks.

@CROCKETT
I know.

@GINA
You stupid, stupid son of a bitch.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - NIGHT - 2330

Crockett and Tubbs, alone, standing too close.

@TUBBS
Six weeks. Six weeks of work and you spent
it on one of ours, who by the way is fine,
and who by the way was never in any danger
we hadn't already walked her into on
purpose.

@CROCKETT
He had her in a van.

@TUBBS
And now he knows who you are and Marta knows
who you are and the only thing standing
between a dead foreman and a dead witness is
whether she thinks you're sentimental.

@CROCKETT
Then she'll think I'm sentimental.

@TUBBS
Good. She should.

@CROCKETT
You want to hit me?

@TUBBS
I want to hit you very much. I'm not going
to, because you'd hit me back and then we'd
both feel better and neither of us would
have learned anything.

@CROCKETT
I learned something.

@TUBBS
What?

@CROCKETT
That I'm not him.

Tubbs looks at him for a long time.

@TUBBS
No. You're not.

INT. HOSPITAL - NIGHT - 0040

Gina on a gurney with an ice pack and a torn dress. TRUDY sitting with her,
still in her undercover earpiece.

@TRUDY
You're off undercover.

@GINA
The hell I am.

@TRUDY
You're off undercover and you're on the
phone, because somebody in that room has to
know the difference between us and them.

@GINA
He put his hands on me.

@TRUDY
I know.

@GINA
He put his hands on me and I didn't get a
sound out.

@TRUDY
Write it down.

@GINA
I don't want to write it down.

@TRUDY
Write it down. That's what we've got that
they don't. We write it down.

EXT. ST. VITUS - MARINA - DAWN - 0605

Crockett burns Burnett's linen jacket in a drum on the dock. The gold cigarette
case goes into the water afterwards. [[CC: Cloth burning; a buoy bell; gulls coming awake.]] [[HOLD: 7]]

He stands there until it's ash, and then he goes below and presses play on the
tape recorder and listens to his own voice saying his own badge number.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - DAY - 1500

A weather map on the television with a hurricane watch crawling along the
bottom of the screen. Phones. Everybody moving at once, the way a squad moves
when it finally has somewhere to go.

@TRUDY
Storm watch for Friday. They moved it up.
Tonight, eleven o'clock, and the truck
leaves at ten forty.

@CASTILLO
Then we're inside at ten thirty and we are
not the ones who knock.

@GINA
(to Castillo)
I'm in.

@CASTILLO
You're at a desk.

@GINA
Lieutenant -

@CASTILLO
You're at a desk with a telephone and a
window, because if you're in that building
and he sees you, you're not an officer any
more, you're a message.

Gina holds that, and nods, and hates it, and nods again.

@GINA
Then put Trudy in the van with me.

@CASTILLO
Done.

INT. OCB - EVIDENCE ROOM - DAY - 1530

Crockett and Tubbs with Eddie Marin's scanned page on the light table, the
photocopy blown up until the numbers are soft.

@TUBBS
Nineteen, thirty-one, forty. Crate numbers.

@CROCKETT
Not crate numbers. Dates. March nineteenth,
March thirty-first, April fortieth - no.
Look. They're every twelve days.

@TUBBS
Twelve days.

@CROCKETT
And underneath - that's a routing number,
and that's a trust account, and that's a
signature line with no name on it.

@TUBBS
He photocopied the whole pipeline and mailed
it to a charity in an envelope with eleven
dollars in it.

@CROCKETT
He mailed it to the one place that couldn't
throw it away.

@TUBBS
Because it's evidence against them.

@CROCKETT
Because it's a donation. Nobody shreds a
donation.

Tubbs looks at the blow-up for a while.

@TUBBS
Nine years of eleven dollars.

@CROCKETT
Nine years of eleven dollars to buy the
right to put a page in an envelope.

INT. OCB - HALLWAY - DAY - 1545

Crockett catches CASTILLO alone by the coffee machine.

@CROCKETT
Switek's wire.

@CASTILLO
What about it.

@CROCKETT
He told Cuba eleven o'clock. If Cuba tells
the warehouse, they'll be waiting.

@CASTILLO
Cuba will tell the warehouse.

@CROCKETT
And we go anyway.

@CASTILLO
We go anyway, because they'll be waiting at
eleven, and we're going in at ten thirty.

Crockett takes that in.

@CROCKETT
You used him.

@CASTILLO
He used himself. I just knew what time he
was going to say.

@CROCKETT
Does Zito know?

@CASTILLO
Zito knows.

INT. HOSPITAL - CORRIDOR - DAY - 1610

ZITO, in a hospital gown with his arm in a sling, signing himself out against a
form and a very unimpressed NURSE.

@NURSE
Detective, you have a through-and-through
and a drain in it.

@ZITO
I have a warehouse.

@NURSE
You have a drain.

@ZITO
Keep the drain. I'll bring it back.

He signs. Switek is standing at the end of the corridor with a paper cup and
nothing to say.

@ZITO
(CONT'D)
You drove me here. You can drive me back.

@SWITEK
Larry -

@ZITO
Not now. Drive.

INT. COUNTY - FAMILY SERVICES - NIGHT - 2130

Rain on the windows. A night SUPERVISOR with a clipboard and a telephone.

@NIGHT SUPERVISOR
(into the telephone)
She was here at six. She was gone by seven.
She took her bag and she took the envelope.

@TUBBS
(into the telephone, taking the phone)
What envelope?

@NIGHT SUPERVISOR
The one she came in with. It had a
photograph in it and a piece of paper. She
wouldn't let anybody touch it.

Tubbs is already moving.

@TUBBS
Did she say where she was going?

@NIGHT SUPERVISOR
She said she was going to be able to say
what was there.

EXT. COUNTY - PARKING LOT - NIGHT - 2145

Rain. Tubbs finds LUPE sitting on the bonnet of his car with her school bag on
her knees, soaked through.

@TUBBS
You ran away from a county office.

@LUPE
I ran to your car.

@TUBBS
That's the same thing to a judge.

@LUPE
Then tell the judge.

@TUBBS
Lupe -

@LUPE
My father counted things. Somebody has to be
able to say what was there. If you leave me
in an office, nobody says anything.

Tubbs looks at her, and at the rain, and at the clock in the dashboard.

@TUBBS
You stay in the car. Locked. Windows up.

@LUPE
Yes.

@TUBBS
Say it again.

@LUPE
Locked. Windows up.

@TUBBS
And if I tell you to drive away, you drive
away and you don't stop until you find a
policeman who isn't scared.

@LUPE
That's my father's line.

@TUBBS
It's a good line.

INT. TELEPHONE BOOTH - OCEAN DRIVE - NIGHT - 2155

Burnett in a telephone booth with the rain coming down and the receiver wet. He
dials. It rings four times.

@MARTA (V.O.)
(filtered)
Mr. Burnett. Or should I ask who's calling?

@CROCKETT
It's been a long week.

@MARTA (V.O.)
It has. A man took a girl out of my loading
bay tonight and I understand a policeman was
involved.

@CROCKETT
A policeman was involved.

@MARTA (V.O.)
Then we're both liars, and one of us is
about to be expensive.

@CROCKETT
What do you want?

@MARTA (V.O.)
I want what I said I wanted on the first
day. I want to know who's been putting
things in my crates. And I want it on a
Wednesday, with a lawyer on a retainer and a
statement already typed.

@CROCKETT
Wednesday's tonight.

@MARTA (V.O.)
Wednesday's tonight. So here it is, Mr.
Burnett, or Detective, whatever you are: I
have a ledger, and it has names in it that
are not mine, and I will trade it to you for
a flight and a head start.

@CROCKETT
Where?

@MARTA (V.O.)
The warehouse. Eleven o'clock. Bring nothing
and bring everybody.

She hangs up. Crockett stands in the booth with the rain coming down.

@CROCKETT
(to himself)
She's the witness.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - NIGHT - 2200

Crockett bursts in. Castillo, Trudy, Gina, Switek, Zito - all of them armed and
armouring up.

@CROCKETT
She's Coburn's witness.

@CASTILLO
Who.

@CROCKETT
Marta Escobar. Coburn's been running her for
twenty-two months against herself.

@TRUDY
That's why the ledger's in the warehouse.
She's been building it for two years.

@ZITO
And we're raiding it.

@CROCKETT
She asked for it. She invited us.

@CASTILLO
Then she's finally scheduled it.

Castillo picks up his radio.

@CASTILLO
(CONT'D)
(into the radio)
Roll it.

EXT. DOCKS - DAY - RAIN - 1640

Wind coming up. Castillo briefing in the lee of a container, six people in
windbreakers with their collars turned.

@CASTILLO
Warehouse, not the strip. The plane lands at
one, and if it lands with the crates on it,
then everything we did was a raid on an
empty room. Nobody goes in alone. Nobody.

He looks at Switek when he says it, and Switek looks at his shoes.

@CASTILLO
(CONT'D)
Switek stays in the van.

@SWITEK
Lieutenant -

@CASTILLO
You're in the van because you're the reason
they think it's eleven, and I want you alive
to explain that to a judge.

@SWITEK
Yes, Lieutenant.

@CASTILLO
Zito. You're with me at the north door and
you're carrying nothing heavier than a radio
until I say different.

@ZITO
Understood.

INT. IZZY'S LOT - LITTLE HAVANA - DAY - 1730

A HANDS OF THE GULF box truck, idling, with Izzy in the cab and a clipboard he
cannot read.

@IZZY
You understand that if I do this, I am not a
criminal. I am a volunteer. Volunteers go to
heaven. Criminals go to a place with worse
coffee.

@CROCKETT
Drive the truck, Izzy. Stop at the door. Get
out. Walk to the fence.

@IZZY
And then?

@CROCKETT
Then keep walking.

@IZZY
How far?

@CROCKETT
Until somebody tells you to stop.

Izzy looks at the truck, then at the rain, then at Crockett.

@IZZY
You know what my mother would say? She'd say
a man who drives a truck into trouble for
nothing is a man who will drive a truck into
trouble for anything.

@CROCKETT
What do you say?

@IZZY
I say my mother's been dead eleven years and
I'm still explaining myself to her. Give me
the clipboard.

EXT. WAREHOUSE - MIAMI RIVER - NIGHT - 2200 - STORM

Rain flat and hard, neon bleeding on wet asphalt, palm fronds flat out. The box
truck noses to the roller door. The door goes up. The truck goes in.

@IZZY
(into the radio, in the cab)
I'm in. I'm in, and there's a man with a
shotgun looking at my clipboard, and I would
like to say for the record that my Spanish
is not as good as I told you it was.

Six people move across the yard in the dark. [[CC: Rain on metal; a roller door; distant thunder; a diesel idling.]] [[HOLD: 8]]

@TRUDY
(into the radio)
He's in. Thirty seconds.

@CASTILLO
(into the radio)
Thirty seconds.

INT. WAREHOUSE - NIGHT - 2206

A cathedral of blue crates and sodium light, rain roaring on the roof. Crockett,
Tubbs, Gina - no. Gina is in the van. Crockett, Tubbs, Castillo, Zito and two
uniforms come in low and spread along the racking.

Zito - shoulder bandaged, shouldn't be here - puts a hand on the racking to
steady himself and keeps going.

Crate nineteen is open. Inside: bandages on top, and under the false bottom,
kilo bricks in plastic, stacked as neatly as a donation.

@TUBBS
(whispering)
Nineteen.

@CROCKETT
(whispering)
Nineteen.

@ZITO
(whispering, into the radio)
Crate nineteen is confirmed.

@TRUDY
(into the radio, in the van)
Confirmed.

@GINA
(into the radio, in the van)
Say it again for the tape.

@ZITO
(into the radio)
Crate nineteen, Hands of the Gulf, false
bottom, suspected narcotics. For the tape.

INT. WAREHOUSE - OFFICE - NIGHT - 2209

Glass walls, a space heater, a filing cabinet. COBURN is already here, with a
satchel, taking a ledger out of the cabinet. MARTA stands at the window
watching the crates with her pearls on.

@MARTA
You said eleven.

@COBURN
I said eleven and I lied, because the storm
moved and so did you.

@MARTA
You're taking it.

@COBURN
I'm taking the ledger and I'm taking the
witness, Mrs. Escobar, and you're taking
whatever a jury decides a woman with your
photographs can afford.

@MARTA
I built sixteen stores.

@COBURN
You built a pipeline with a cross on it.
That's not building. That's advertising.

@MARTA
And you let it run. Twenty-two months. You
let eleven tons of somebody's bandages and
nine hundred kilos of their product go
through my warehouse so that I could write
it all down for you.

@COBURN
And you wrote it down.

@MARTA
I wrote it down. And then I invited the
police.

Coburn stops with the ledger half in the satchel.

@COBURN
You invited -

@MARTA
I invited them. Because I have been a
witness for two years and I have worked out
what a witness is. A witness is a person a
policeman keeps in a burning building
because the fire makes good evidence.

From outside, a shout. Then a radio. Then the sound of a roller door coming
down hard.

@COBURN
(to himself)
Ten thirty.

INT. WAREHOUSE - NIGHT - 2212

PRIETO walks the row and stops at a boot sticking out from behind a pallet. He
lifts the tarp. He finds the wire. He finds the man under it.

Prieto smiles the way a man smiles when he has been right about something.

@PRIETO
Policeman.

EXT. WAREHOUSE - LOADING BAY - NIGHT - 2214 - RAIN

The roller door comes down and the shooting starts. Castillo takes the catwalk
with a radio. Zito shoves a uniform behind a pallet and goes down on one knee
and fires once, carefully, because there's a truck full of people behind the
shooter. [[CC: Gunfire in an enclosed space; rain on a metal roof; shouting; a siren winding up.]] [[HOLD: 9]]

@CASTILLO
(into the radio)
North door! North door, they're moving the
truck!

@TRUDY
(into the radio)
Van, we've got one coming out the north door
on foot.

@GINA
(into the radio)
Describe him.

@TRUDY
Grey suit. Carrying a bag.

@GINA
That's the federal man.

INT. WAREHOUSE - NIGHT - 2218

Crockett and Prieto in the racking aisle, both out, both close. It ends with
Prieto on his back between two crates, looking up at nothing, and Crockett on
his knees beside him with a hand on his chest.

@CROCKETT
Who gave the boy the bag?

Prieto says something Crockett does not repeat to anybody. Then he stops.

Crockett stays there for a moment longer than he needs to. Then he gets up.

EXT. WAREHOUSE - NIGHT - 2222 - STORM

Coburn comes out of the side door with the satchel, sees the yard full of
police, and goes instead to the car parked closest to the fence - TUBBS' car.

He gets in. And in the back seat, LUPE, with her school bag on her knees.

@TUBBS
She's in the car.

@CROCKETT
I see it.

@TUBBS
She's in the -

@CROCKETT
I SEE IT.

The car goes out through the gate in a sheet of water. [[CC: An engine; a siren in the distance; thunder; a radio squelching.]]

@TUBBS
(into the radio)
He's got my car. He's got my car and he's
got the girl.

@CASTILLO
(into the radio)
Go. Take Crockett. I've got the building.

INT. TUBBS' CAR - NIGHT - 2225 - RAIN

Crockett drives. Tubbs is on the radio, and then off it, and then holding on
with both hands.

@TUBBS
He's not running. He's driving to a
courthouse. He's going to walk in with a
ledger and a witness and be the man who
saved both.

@CROCKETT
He's going to walk in with a child.

@TUBBS
Sonny. He's going to walk in with a child
and say she's the reason he did it.

@CROCKETT
Then we stop him on the causeway.

@TUBBS
There's nowhere else to stop anybody in this
city.

EXT. MACARTHUR CAUSEWAY - NIGHT - 2231 - RAIN

Coburn's stolen car stops at the slow lane, hazard lights ticking - the same
spot, the same way the film began, with the same water on the other side.

Tubbs gets out with his gun on him and walks up to the driver's window in the
rain. Crockett comes around the other side and gets LUPE out of the back.

@TUBBS
Get out of the car.

@COBURN
There's a girl in this car and there's a
ledger in this car, and when the newspapers
come, you're going to have to say which one
you pointed a gun at.

@TUBBS
Get out of the car.

@COBURN
You shoot me, she's an orphan with a story
nobody can prove. You don't shoot me, she's
a witness with a policeman who didn't. I
have thought about this for two years.

@TUBBS
So have I.

@COBURN
Then you know I'm right.

@TUBBS
I know you're right. Get out of the car.

Tubbs has the shot. He has it for a long time, and the rain comes down, and the
traffic goes past them going the other way, and he does not take it.

He lowers the gun.

EXT. MACARTHUR CAUSEWAY - NIGHT - 2240 - RAIN

Coburn in the back of a patrol car, hands behind him, saying nothing at all.
Lupe on the tailgate with a blanket and Tubbs' jacket over her. The satchel on
the bonnet of another car, in an evidence bag, in the rain.

@LUPE
Is he a bad man?

@TUBBS
He's a policeman.

Lupe considers this, and decides something about the world.

@LUPE
Then who counts the policemen?

Tubbs looks at the water for a while.

@TUBBS
Somebody does. I don't know who.

@LUPE
My father would have known.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - DAWN - 0530

Everybody wet, nobody asleep. The ledger in an evidence bag on the table. A
federal PROSECUTOR, 40s, with a dry umbrella and a warrant.

@PROSECUTOR
That's a federal exhibit.

@CASTILLO
That's dead man's arithmetic in an eleven
dollar envelope. Sign for it.

@PROSECUTOR
Lieutenant, I'm not your enemy here.

@CASTILLO
Then sign for it and prove it.

He signs. Castillo watches him sign, and does not sit down until it's done.

INT. OCB - CASTILLO'S OFFICE - DAWN - 0600

@CASTILLO
(to the prosecutor)
You can have the ledger on one condition.
The child goes into a programme with a name
I pick, not one you pick, and she does not
testify in front of anybody with a camera.

@PROSECUTOR
She's a material witness.

@CASTILLO
She's twelve. Take the ledger.

@PROSECUTOR
And the federal agent?

@CASTILLO
The federal agent is your problem.

@PROSECUTOR
He's going to say he was running a witness.

@CASTILLO
He was. He just stopped being able to tell
the difference between the witness and the
case.

INT. HOSPITAL - WAITING ROOM - DAY - 0900

Zito in surgery. SWITEK in a plastic chair with his tie off and a paper cup of
coffee gone cold, and the bet slips folded in his breast pocket.

A SURGEON comes out with her mask down.

@SURGEON
He'll keep the arm.

@SWITEK
Thank you.

@SURGEON
He asked me to tell you he's not signing
anything for six weeks.

Switek laughs, and it comes out wrong, and the surgeon very kindly pretends it
didn't.

EXT. HOSPITAL - PARKING LOT - DAY - 0930

Rain stopped, steam coming off the asphalt. Switek puts the bet slips in a
trash can, then takes them out, then puts them back.

CASTILLO, across the lot, watches him do it. [[CC: Traffic; a generator; gulls; a car alarm two rows over.]] [[HOLD: 6]]

INT. OCB BULLPEN - DAY - 1000

Switek at Castillo's door, and Zito out of surgery and leaning in the doorway,
and none of it making it any easier.

@SWITEK
Eight months. Three hundred a week. And the
last two weeks I told him what we were
doing, because if I didn't tell him he'd
have broken something of mine.

@CASTILLO
What did he do with it?

@SWITEK
He moved his truck, sir. Twice.

Castillo writes it down. It takes a while.

@CASTILLO
And the two thousand dollars?

@SWITEK
In my locker. In an envelope. I never opened
it.

@CASTILLO
Why not?

@SWITEK
Because I knew what it was for and I wanted
to be able to say I hadn't spent it.

Castillo finishes writing.

@CASTILLO
Badge and gun. Six months, then a hearing.
You'll be at a desk with no telephone.

@SWITEK
Thank you, Lieutenant.

@CASTILLO
Don't. There's nothing to thank me for and
you know it.

@SWITEK
Yes, Lieutenant.

Zito waits until Switek is gone.

@ZITO
Six months.

@CASTILLO
Six months and a hearing. He'll be back at a
desk with a telephone in a year.

@ZITO
That's generous.

@CASTILLO
He moved a truck twice, Larry. That's the
only thing he did that mattered, and it's
the only thing that was ever his to give.

EXT. IZZY'S LOT - LITTLE HAVANA - DAY - 1100

Crockett leans on the fender of the Cadillac. Izzy has a new cigar and the same
shirt, and a bandage on his forearm he is very proud of.

@IZZY
So. The reward.

@CROCKETT
There isn't one.

@IZZY
I know. I drove a truck into a warehouse
full of men with guns for a city that spells
my name wrong on the parking tickets.

@CROCKETT
You did good, Izzy.

@IZZY
I did good. Write it down. That's what
you people do, you write it down.

He looks at the sky, which is clearing.

@IZZY
(CONT'D)
Next time, I'm a volunteer for somebody
else's charity. The ones with cookies.

INT. HANDS OF THE GULF - MARTA'S OFFICE - DAY - 1130

Marta at her desk with the pearls and the photographs and a box of her own
files going out the door. CASTILLO and TRUDY with a warrant.

@MARTA
You'll want to know about the bishop.

@CASTILLO
I don't want to know about the bishop.

@MARTA
Everyone wants to know about the bishop.

Trudy lifts the framed photograph of the dock children.

@TRUDY
Who are they?

@MARTA
Children on a dock, Detective. They're
children on a dock. That's what makes the
whole thing work.

@CASTILLO
Eleven tons.

@MARTA
Eleven tons. And nine hundred kilos, and I
wrote down every one of them, and I want
that in the record, because when you stand
up in a courtroom and tell a jury what I am,
somebody should be able to say what else I
was.

@CASTILLO
(to Trudy)
Put it in the record.

@MARTA
I'd like a lawyer now.

@CASTILLO
You'll need one.

INT. FEDERAL BUILDING - CONFERENCE ROOM - DAY - 1400

The prosecutor slides a paper across to COBURN, who reads it like a man reading
his own obituary and finding it flattering.

@COBURN
Time served and a pension.

@PROSECUTOR
You'll testify against Escobar.

@COBURN
I'll testify.

@PROSECUTOR
And against Prieto's people.

@COBURN
Prieto's dead.

@PROSECUTOR
Prieto's people aren't.

Coburn signs. Outside the window, Miami is having a beautiful afternoon.

@COBURN
Do you want to know the worst part?

@PROSECUTOR
No.

@COBURN
The worst part is that I was right. Twenty-
two months and I was right the whole time.
She walked out of that building and I have
got a case that will put a woman in prison
for nine hundred kilos and eleven tons.

@PROSECUTOR
That's not the worst part. That's the job.

@COBURN
No. The worst part is that she's the one who
called you.

EXT. OCEAN DRIVE - NIGHT - 2100

Neon on wet street. A black convertible at the kerb with the top down, CROCKETT
at the wheel, TUBBS beside him, neither of them going anywhere.

@TUBBS
She asked me who counts the policemen.

@CROCKETT
What did you tell her?

@TUBBS
I told her somebody does.

@CROCKETT
Does somebody?

@TUBBS
Eddie Marin counted crates for nine years
for eleven dollars a month and then put a
page in an envelope and died in a box for
it. So. Somebody.

EXT. OCEAN DRIVE - NIGHT - 2106 - CONTINUOUS

The same two men, the same car, the same street.

@CROCKETT
We got the warehouse. We got the plane. We
got a woman who built sixteen stores on
bandages and a federal agent who let half of
it through to keep her breathing.

@TUBBS
And we didn't get the man who handed a
nineteen-year-old a bag, because Prieto took
that with him.

@CROCKETT
We got the girl.

@TUBBS
We got the girl.

They sit with it, and the neon goes through a whole cycle in the puddle beside
the car. [[HOLD: 8]]

@CROCKETT
You know what she said to me? In the booth.
She said the truth isn't a thing you find.
It's a thing you schedule.

@TUBBS
She's not wrong.

@CROCKETT
No. She's not wrong. That's what's so
fucking depressing about it.

INT. MARIN HOUSEHOLD - LITTLE HAVANA - NIGHT - 2215

Tubbs puts Eddie Marin's watch on the kitchen table in front of LUPE. The house
is the same. The envelope is still unopened.

@TUBBS
He said give it to a policeman who wasn't
scared.

@LUPE
Are you one?

@TUBBS
I'm one who's scared.

She picks it up, winds it, and holds it to her ear to hear if it works.

@LUPE
Then he picked the wrong one.

She puts it on anyway. Then she opens her father's pay envelope for the first
time, counts it, and puts it back without a word.

@TUBBS
You want to come and stay with my sister for
a while? Just until the paperwork.

@LUPE
Is she a policeman?

@TUBBS
No.

@LUPE
Then yes.

EXT. ST. VITUS - MARINA - NIGHT - 2300

Crockett on the transom with Burnett's passport in his hand. He opens it, looks
at the photograph for a while, and drops it in the water.

Then he goes below, takes the tape out of the recorder, and drops that in too. [[CC: Water; a buoy bell; distant music from Ocean Drive; a diesel starting somewhere.]] [[HOLD: 8]]

INT. OCB BULLPEN - NIGHT - 2330

TRUDY typing the report with two fingers and a coffee. GINA at a desk with a
sling and a telephone, answering a board she can't reach.

@GINA
(to the phone)
Vice. No. No, ma'am, we can't help you with
that. That's a very nice dog.

She hangs up.

@GINA
(CONT'D)
Seventy-one pages.

@TRUDY
Seventy-one pages and none of them say what
happened.

@GINA
Put in the part about the truck leaving at
three.

@TRUDY
It's in.

@GINA
And the part about the boy behind the truck?

@TRUDY
Alberto Ruiz is in. They found him at the
strip this morning. Twenty-four. Eighty
dollars.

Neither of them says anything for a bit. Then Trudy types again.

EXT. ROOFTOP - DOWNTOWN - NIGHT - 2345

CASTILLO alone above the city with the case files under his arm. He looks at
Miami the way a man looks at a thing he has agreed to keep.

Behind him, the door opens. TRUDY comes out with two coffees and hands him one,
and stands beside him without saying anything, which is the right thing to do.

@CASTILLO
Seventy-one pages.

@TRUDY
Seventy-two now.

@CASTILLO
Seventy-two.

They drink the coffee and look at the city. [[HOLD: 10]]

EXT. MACARTHUR CAUSEWAY - DAWN - 0610

The black convertible going east into the light, top down, hazard lights
ticking, the way the truck did at the beginning.

EXT. BEACH - MIAMI BEACH - DAWN - 0630

Crockett on the sand in his shoes, fully dressed, watching the water come up
and go back out. Nobody with him. He takes off the gold chain and puts it in his
pocket. [[HOLD: 10]]

INT. OCB BULLPEN - DAY - 0900

The board wiped. TRUDY writes a new name on it. The telephone rings and
somebody answers it.

@GINA
(into the telephone)
Vice. Yes, ma'am. Yes, we can help you with
that.

INT. OCB BULLPEN - DAY - 0910 - TAG

SWITEK at a desk with no telephone, opening a filing cabinet that has never
been opened. ZITO, in a sling, leans on the cabinet.

@ZITO
They take the telephone out?

@SWITEK
They took the telephone out.

@ZITO
Six months.

@SWITEK
Six months.

Zito hands him a coffee.

@ZITO
Bet you two hundred you can't do it.

@SWITEK
I'm not taking that bet.

@ZITO
Good.

EXT. WAREHOUSE - MIAMI RIVER - DAY - 0915

Sun on the blue crates, which are still there, stacked, waiting for somebody
else's paperwork. A new padlock on the roller door. [[CC: Gulls; a distant forklift; a truck on the highway.]]

EXT. CEMETERY - DAY - 1000

A small service. Five people. LUPE puts a photocopy of her father's numbers on
the grave and weights it with a stone, so it won't blow away.

TUBBS stands back with his hands in his pockets, and does not cry, because
that's not what he does in front of a twelve-year-old.

@LUPE
(to the grave)
Nineteen.

She says it out loud, so somebody can hear it.

EXT. MARINA - DAY - 1100

The ST. VITUS leaves her slip with Crockett at the wheel and nobody else
aboard. [[CC: A diesel engine; rigging; gulls; a radio playing something with horns in it.]]

EXT. BISCAYNE BAY - DAY - 1130

The boat well out, the city small and white behind it, the water doing what
water does.

A radio crackles. Crockett looks at it for a moment before he answers.

@CROCKETT
(into the radio)
Go ahead.

@DISPATCH (O.S.)
(filtered)
Boat. We've got one on the river.

He looks back at the city once, and then he turns the wheel and takes it. [[HOLD: 12]]

FADE OUT.
